course: chemistry
lesson: 27
title: Dyes, inks and colouring
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Squash a blueberry on your T-shirt and the purple mark refuses to wash out. The berry's colour molecules have stuck to the threads of the cloth: that is exactly what a dye does.
explain: Dyes dissolve and soak into things like cloth, paper or hair. Pigments are coloured powders that do not dissolve; they are mixed with a sort of glue and sit on top, as paint does on a wall.
explain: For thousands of years colours came from plants, snails, insects and coloured earth, and some were so hard to make that only kings could pay for them. Today most dyes are made in factories, and that began with a lucky accident in 1856.
explain: Dyes can also leave: they wash out, rub off and fade in sunlight. That is why jeans go pale and why one red sock can turn a whole wash pink.
fact: A dye is a coloured substance whose molecules stick to fibres; berry stains are dyes from the fruit.
fact: Fresh stains come out more easily than old ones, because the colour has had less time to hold on to the fibres.
fact: Felt-tip pens hold dyes dissolved in a liquid; the liquid soaks into the paper and dries, leaving the dye; black felt-tips often contain several colours mixed.
fact: Tyrian purple came from thousands of sea snails for a small amount of dye, so it was so dear that it became the colour of emperors and kings.
fact: In 1856 William Perkin, aged 18, was trying to make a medicine and made the first cheap factory-made purple dye by accident.
fact: Onion skins boiled in water release a natural dye that colours eggs brown or golden.
fact: Paint is made of pigments, coloured powders that do not dissolve, mixed with a binder that glues them to the surface; cave painters used coloured earth and charcoal.
fact: Jeans are dyed with indigo, which sits mostly on the outside of the threads, so each wash rubs a little off and the jeans fade.
fact: Indigo was once made from plants; now it is usually made in factories.
fact: Some hair dyes open the outer layer of the hair so the colour can get inside and react there; because some people are allergic, a skin test is done first.
fact: Food colourings are tested to be safe in small amounts; they add looks, not taste, and some come from plants such as beetroot.
fact: Sunlight's ultraviolet rays break dye molecules apart, so curtains and posters fade near a window and museums keep the lights low.
fact: A red sock can leak loose dye into the wash water, and the dye then sticks to other clothes; colour-catcher sheets soak up loose dye.
exercise: Why is a blueberry stain hard to wash out of a T-shirt? | Its colour molecules stick to the threads | The berry juice slowly turns into a kind of plastic | The stain is on the inside of your skin | Blueberry juice is a kind of paint
exercise: Why should a fresh stain be washed sooner rather than later? | Fresh stains come out more easily than old ones | Old stains just disappear on their own after a while | Fresh stains spread to the other clothes | Old stains are easier to bleach out
exercise: What happens to the ink of a felt-tip pen on paper? | The liquid soaks in and dries, leaving the dye | The ink stays wet on top forever | The ink turns into tiny pieces of plastic | The paper soaks up the colour and the liquid stays
exercise: Why was purple once a colour only for kings? | Its dye came from thousands of sea snails | The law said purple was a royal flower for crowns | Purple was the colour of gold coins | Purple dye was poison to ordinary people
exercise: What was William Perkin trying to make when he found a cheap purple dye? | A medicine | A sweet | A firework | A soap
exercise: How can onion skins colour Easter eggs? | Boiling them lets out a natural brown dye | The dry skins are glued one by one onto the eggshells | The onion smell turns the eggs brown | The eggs are painted with onion juice
exercise: How is a pigment different from a dye? | A pigment does not dissolve and sits on top | A pigment is always made from crushed sea snails | A pigment soaks deep into every fibre | A pigment is a dye that has gone dry
exercise: What did cave painters use for their colours? | Coloured earth and charcoal | Felt-tip pens and crayons | Berries mixed with sea water | Factory-made dyes from shops
exercise: Why do blue jeans fade after many washes? | The indigo on the outside of the threads rubs off | The water bleaches them a little bit more every time | The blue sinks to the inside of the threads | The washing machine soaks up blue light
exercise: Why is a skin test done before using hair dye? | Some people are allergic to it | It shows which colour will suit you | It warms the skin so the dye works | It checks if the hair is clean
exercise: Why are food colourings put in icing? | For looks, not for taste | To make the icing sweeter | To stop the icing going off | To make the icing harder
exercise: Why do curtains near a sunny window fade? | Ultraviolet rays in sunlight break dye molecules | The heat melts the threads of the cloth | Sunlight washes the colour onto the glass | The window glass soaks up the colour
exercise: Why did a white shirt turn pink in the wash? | A red sock leaked dye that stuck to it | Hot water always turns white cotton cloth pink | The washing powder had red bits in it | The shirt was blushing from the heat
exercise: What does a colour-catcher sheet do in the wash? | It soaks up loose dye in the water | It adds new colour to faded clothes | It makes white clothes brighter blue | It stops the water from getting hot
exercise: Why do museums keep old paintings and fabrics in dim light? | Light slowly breaks down their colours | Bright light makes paintings melt | Visitors see colours better in the dark | Dim light keeps the room smelling fresh
exercise: Where is indigo dye usually made today? | In factories | From sea snails | From onion skins | From blueberries
test: Mia: "jeans fade cos the blue sinks deep inside the threads". What's true? | The blue sits on the outside and rubs off | She's right, it sinks to the middle of each thread | The blue is eaten by the soap | The jeans dry out and lose colour
test: Dad, holding a pink shirt: "who put a bloody red sock in with the whites?" What happened? | Loose red dye moved onto the white shirt | The hot water turned the white shirt red all over | The shirt soaked up red light | The machine was painted inside
test: If you hung your favourite poster in a sunny window for a year, what would happen? | Its colours would fade | Its colours would get brighter | It would turn completely black | Nothing at all would change
test: A cartoon king wears purple because "purple was the cheapest dye". What's wrong? | Purple dye was rare and very dear | Purple was banned for kings by the law | Kings only ever wore gold | Purple was made from onions
test: "pigment and dye are the same thing, just colour, innit". What's the difference? | A dye dissolves and soaks in, a pigment sits on top | A pigment is liquid, a dye is a powder | A dye is natural, a pigment is not | There is no difference at all
test: You colour with a black felt-tip and drip water on it, and other colours appear. Why? | Black felt-tip ink is a mix of colours | Water makes new colours out of nothing | The paper had hidden colours inside | Water turns black ink into rainbow ink
test: Ella's aunt dyes her hair at home without reading the box. What should she have done first? | A skin test for allergy | Wash the dye in cold water | Leave the dye in the sun | Mix two dyes together
test: Ben says food colouring makes red icing taste of strawberries. What's true? | Colouring adds looks, not taste | He's right, red always tastes of strawberry | Colouring makes icing sour | Colouring makes icing taste of beetroot
test: Which is a natural dye? | Onion skins boiled in water | Indigo from a factory | Perkin's purple from 1856 | Ink from a felt-tip pen
test: What would happen if Perkin hadn't had his lucky accident? | Cheap factory purple would have come later | Nobody would ever be able to see purple again | Kings would have worn green instead | Snails would have died out
test: "spilt blueberry smoothie on me top, I'll wash it next week". Good plan? | No, fresh stains wash out more easily | Yes, old stains come out easier | Yes, the stain will fade by itself | No, it should be put in the sun first
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a dye and a pigment? | A dye dissolves and is absorbed, whereas a pigment is insoluble and remains on the surface | A dye is invariably natural, whereas a pigment is invariably synthetic | A dye is a solid, whereas a pigment is a gas | There exists no distinction between them
exam: Identify the principal cause of the fading of textiles exposed to sunlight. | The decomposition of dye molecules by ultraviolet radiation | The evaporation of the fibres in warm air | The absorption of colour by window glass | The bleaching action of moisture alone
exam: It is given that William Perkin, in 1856, produced a purple dye. Select the statement consistent with the established facts. | The discovery was accidental, during an attempt to make a medicine | The dye was extracted from sea snails | The discovery was made at an advanced age | The dye was obtained from onion skins
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a white garment acquires a pink colour when laundered with a red sock. | Loose dye is released into the water and attaches to the garment | The temperature of the water converts white to pink | The detergent contains a red pigment | The garment reflects the colour of the sock
